WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:28.110
With that boyish face, slick hair, slight
frame and pouty lips Audie Murphy could have

00:00:28.110 --> 00:00:31.430
easily been one of those heartthrobs from
the James Dean era.

00:00:31.430 --> 00:00:34.870
A rebel without a cause, a film star or a
pop recording artist.

00:00:34.870 --> 00:00:38.030
He was, in fact, all of the above and then
some.

00:00:38.030 --> 00:00:42.010
Audie Murphy was the real deal, one who could
talk the talk and walk the walk.

00:00:42.010 --> 00:00:47.080
Aged barely 21, 2nd Lieutenant Audie Murphy
had fought his way from North Africa to France

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:53.190
in WWII, becoming the most decorated soldier
in US History – and a badass Axis-killing

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:54.190
machine.

00:00:54.190 --> 00:00:58.800
In today’s Biographics video we will look
at the life of Audie Murphy and how he went

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:01.360
To Hell and Back.

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:09.320
1$ a day
Audie Leon Murphy was born on the 20th of

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:14.720
June 1925 in Kingston, Hunt County, Texas
in a family of poor sharecroppers of Irish

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:15.720
descent.

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:16.970
He did not have an easy life to start with.

00:01:16.970 --> 00:01:21.640
Audie had to share a home with 11 siblings
with no abundance of food on the table.

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:25.810
Audie’s father, Emmett was not a model dad,
by any means, neglecting to provide for his

00:01:25.810 --> 00:01:28.200
wife Josie Bell and their 12 children.

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:32.340
Young Audie felt a strong responsibility to
care for his family and quit school at an

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:34.530
early age, managing to complete only 5th grade.

00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:39.159
He went to work in the Texas cotton fields,
earning a reputation as an indefatigable cotton

00:01:39.159 --> 00:01:45.430
picker, which was well rewarded with the princely
pay of 1$ a day, 18$ in today’s money.

00:01:45.430 --> 00:01:49.250
We don’t know who his employer was, but
I’ll take a guess and call him Stingy Bastard,

00:01:49.250 --> 00:01:50.250
if you don’t mind.

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:53.619
There was little food you could buy with that
kind of money, so Audie had to resort to additional

00:01:53.619 --> 00:01:55.030
means of sustenance.

00:01:55.030 --> 00:01:59.229
At the age in which kids in the US nowadays
hunt for cute critters with their Pokemon

00:01:59.229 --> 00:02:03.450
Go app, Audie Murphy also went hunting for
cute critters - with his rifle.

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:08.590
Squirrels, rabbits and other small game fell
under his shots and then onto Mum Josie’s

00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:09.700
kitchen worktop.

00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:13.760
The experience enabled Murphy to develop great
marksmanship skills, which would later prove

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:16.060
useful against another type of prey.

00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:20.590
Growing into his teenage years, Audie looked
for other odd jobs, working as shop clerk

00:02:20.590 --> 00:02:22.200
and gas station attendant.

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:28.590
I expect the pay was higher than 1$ a day,
but things went from bad to worse when in

00:02:28.590 --> 00:02:31.590
1940 Emmett Murphy upped sticks and deserted
his family.

00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:34.540
Audie’s mother Josie Bell died of pneumonia
the following year.

00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:41.280
Suddenly Audie felt the weight of his family’s
welfare entirely on his narrow and frail shoulders.

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:48.570
Joining the Army
When the US were dragged into WWII by Japan’s

00:02:48.570 --> 00:02:53.370
surprise attack on Pearl Harbour, 7th of December
1941, Audie Murphy thought “that’s it,

00:02:53.370 --> 00:02:56.410
that’s what I am gonna do, I will join the
military!”.

00:02:56.410 --> 00:03:00.889
He saw this not only as a means to provide
for his siblings, but also as a way to honour

00:03:00.889 --> 00:03:01.889
his mother’s memory.

00:03:01.889 --> 00:03:02.889
There was a slight problem, though.

00:03:02.889 --> 00:03:03.889
Audie tried to enlist first with the Marines,
then with the paratroopers, but those elite

00:03:03.889 --> 00:03:06.000
corps turned him down due to his diminutive
size and weak frame.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:12.819
Audie in fact stood at only 5 foot 5 and weighed
a mere 110 pounds (that’s166 cm and 45 kilos)

00:03:12.819 --> 00:03:16.460
– but he wasn’t going to accept such an
early setback.

00:03:16.460 --> 00:03:20.350
It took him seven months, but he was able
to gain 12 pounds in weight.

00:03:20.350 --> 00:03:23.790
Armed with this added bulk and his sister
false testimony that he was 18 – he was

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:27.130
actually 17 – he finally was inducted into
the military.

00:03:27.130 --> 00:03:32.040
Not in the Marines, his armed force of choice,
but in the regular infantry.

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:36.260
After basic training at Camp Wolters, Texas,
and advanced training at Fort Meade, Maryland,

00:03:36.260 --> 00:03:40.670
Audie was assigned to the famous 15th Infantry
Regiment and shipped off to North Africa.

00:03:40.670 --> 00:03:45.639
The 15th landed in French Morocco in November
1942 as part of Operation Torch.

00:03:45.639 --> 00:03:49.320
This consisted in attacking from the West
the Axis forces in North Africa, while the

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:53.780
British and Commonwealth forces were squeezing
the Italo-Germans from the East.

00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:58.510
This was the first campaign in which American
forces would engage the Axis on land.

00:03:58.510 --> 00:04:03.070
After initial setbacks at the Kasserine pass,
the Allied divisions, slowly but surely, encircled

00:04:03.070 --> 00:04:05.290
the Italo-German armies in Tunisia.

00:04:05.290 --> 00:04:15.010
They eventually surrendered in May 13, 1943
To Hell …

00:04:15.010 --> 00:04:18.600
During the Tunisian Campaign Audie Murphy
did not saw combat.

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:22.729
But this was about to change: his first experience
of actual fighting occurred after the Allied

00:04:22.729 --> 00:04:25.400
invasion of Sicily, in July 1943.

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:29.740
In a skirmish against Italian soldiers, Murphy
put to good use his natural hunting talent

00:04:29.740 --> 00:04:31.370
and shot dead two officers.

00:04:31.370 --> 00:04:35.530
This action earned him the first of many battlefield
promotions becoming a Corporal.

00:04:35.530 --> 00:04:40.320
During the Italian campaign Audie consistently
impressed his superiors thanks to his courage,

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:42.270
resilience and innate leadership skills.

00:04:42.270 --> 00:04:44.910
On one occasion he was hit by a German sniper
on the hip.

00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:48.690
He did not lose his cool and returned fire,
hitting the sniper between the eyes.

00:04:48.690 --> 00:04:52.460
But the only foes that managed to hold him
back in Italy were mosquitoes, as he spent

00:04:52.460 --> 00:04:56.180
much time in a field hospital due to several
bouts of malaria.

00:04:56.180 --> 00:05:01.060
In August 1944 Audie and the 15th Regiment
were in Southern France as part of Operation

00:05:01.060 --> 00:05:02.060
Dragoon.

00:05:02.060 --> 00:05:06.280
One day, Audie and his unit, which included
his best friend Lattie Tipton were lured out

00:05:06.280 --> 00:05:08.720
of their fox hole by a group of surrendering
Germans.

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:09.860
It was a trap.

00:05:09.860 --> 00:05:11.820
One of the Germans shot at Lattie, killing
him.

00:05:11.820 --> 00:05:13.210
Audie simply went berserk.

00:05:13.210 --> 00:05:17.720
He charged at the German squad and wiped them
out single-handedly.

00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:21.520
He then grabbed their heavy machine gun and
grenades and put them to good use, attacking

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:25.190
several more German positions, killing all
enemy soldiers in the area.

00:05:25.190 --> 00:05:30.290
Murphy received his first important decoration,
the Distinguished Service Cross, in recognition

00:05:30.290 --> 00:05:31.500
of these actions.

00:05:31.500 --> 00:05:33.550
This was a consistent trait of Murphy’s.

00:05:33.550 --> 00:05:38.050
He would charge into battle in the most desperate
circumstances, always emerging alive – albeit

00:05:38.050 --> 00:05:42.300
not unscathed – after inflicting tremendous
damage to the enemy, thanks to his nerves

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:44.650
of steel and excellent marksmanship.

00:05:44.650 --> 00:05:49.120
As a reward, Murphy not only collected an
impressive number of medals, but he also escalated

00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:50.120
the ranks.

00:05:50.120 --> 00:05:55.250
By January 1945, aged barely 20, Audie had
moved from Private to Corporal to Sergeant

00:05:55.250 --> 00:05:56.250
to 2nd Lieutenant.

00:05:56.250 --> 00:06:01.770
It was on the 26th of that month that 2nd
Lieutenant Audie Murphy performed the action

00:06:01.770 --> 00:06:04.630
that would make him a legend for the years
to come.

00:06:04.630 --> 00:06:08.540
In the early afternoon of that day, Murphy
was leading a platoon of some 40 U.S. troops

00:06:08.540 --> 00:06:11.560
in a clearing near the French town of Holtzwihr,
close to the German border.

00:06:11.560 --> 00:06:14.890
The unit was charged with holding a roadway
until reinforcements arrived.

00:06:14.890 --> 00:06:20.110
But the relief force did not materialise and
just after 2 p.m., the worst happened: 250

00:06:20.110 --> 00:06:24.630
German troops supported by six tanks and an
artillery barrage emerged from the woods and

00:06:24.630 --> 00:06:25.630
attacked.

00:06:25.630 --> 00:06:28.950
Murphy’s first instinct was to get his men
to safety and so he instructed them to withdraw

00:06:28.950 --> 00:06:32.050
to dug-in defensive positions along a nearby
tree line.

00:06:32.050 --> 00:06:36.660
As they ran for cover, he stayed behind and
used his field telephone to call in an artillery

00:06:36.660 --> 00:06:37.660
strike.

00:06:37.660 --> 00:06:41.450
He managed to do so just in time, before the
Panzers opened fire against his position,

00:06:41.450 --> 00:06:45.360
destroying a machine gun nest and setting
an American tank destroyer on fire.

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:49.330
But if there is something US soldiers can
rely upon is their artillery.

00:06:49.330 --> 00:06:53.130
In few seconds, a barrage of shells exploded
between Murphy and the Germans.

00:06:53.130 --> 00:06:57.180
The open field, which had been covered in
immaculate snow a few minutes before was now

00:06:57.180 --> 00:06:59.750
a hellish landscape of craters.

00:06:59.750 --> 00:07:03.680
Murphy took advantage of the cover offered
by the barrage and retreated atop the burning

00:07:03.680 --> 00:07:04.680
tank destroyer.

00:07:04.680 --> 00:07:08.800
Over the radio, the artillery commander asked
him how close the Germans were to his position.

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:11.000
He replied
“Just hold the phone and I’ll let you

00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:12.480
talk to one of the bastards!”

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:17.150
Squad after squad of Germans seemed intent
on having a chat on the phone with the artillery

00:07:17.150 --> 00:07:20.990
guy … but Murphy had found out that the
.50-caliber machine gun on top of the tank

00:07:20.990 --> 00:07:22.600
destroyer was still operational.

00:07:22.600 --> 00:07:27.081
He quickly opened fire, burst after burst,
mowing down dozens of enemy soldiers, all

00:07:27.081 --> 00:07:29.950
the while directing artillery fire over the
radio.

00:07:29.950 --> 00:07:33.640
German gunners fought back with small arms
and tank fire, with the shrapnel of one blast

00:07:33.640 --> 00:07:34.870
wounding Murphy in the leg.

00:07:34.870 --> 00:07:36.550
As usual, he kept on fighting.

00:07:36.550 --> 00:07:40.810
It was only when the ammo on the machine gun
ran out that Murphy finally jumped from the

00:07:40.810 --> 00:07:43.200
flaming tank destroyer and went back to his
men.

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:47.860
He later wrote that his only thought while
retreating was: “How come I’m not dead?”

00:07:47.860 --> 00:07:51.540
Murphy had now reached safety behind the defensive
positions set up by his men.

00:07:51.540 --> 00:07:55.430
He had personally killed or wounded about
50 enemies and taken out dozens more indirectly

00:07:55.430 --> 00:07:57.050
via the artillery strike.

00:07:57.050 --> 00:07:58.520
But he wasn’t done yet.

00:07:58.520 --> 00:08:03.730
Murphy refused to be evacuated from the battlefield
and instead rallied his company into counterattacking

00:08:03.730 --> 00:08:07.050
the Germans, finally driving them back into
the woods.

00:08:07.050 --> 00:08:11.940
2nd Lieutenant Audie Murphy was awarded the
Medal of Honour for his actions at Holtzwihr.

00:08:11.940 --> 00:08:14.230
The official citation which came with the
Medal reads

00:08:14.230 --> 00:08:18.980
“Murphy's indomitable courage and his refusal
to give an inch of ground saved his company

00:08:18.980 --> 00:08:23.760
from possible encirclement and destruction,
and enabled it to hold the woods which had

00:08:23.760 --> 00:08:25.370
been the enemy's objective.”

00:08:25.370 --> 00:08:28.810
Audie had now become something of a celebrity
among the Allied troops and his commanders

00:08:28.810 --> 00:08:33.139
would rather have him as a liaison officer
rather than having risk his life on the front

00:08:33.139 --> 00:08:34.139
line.

00:08:34.139 --> 00:08:36.669
The last weeks of the war were relatively
peaceful for him – not that he needed to

00:08:36.669 --> 00:08:37.810
see more actions.

00:08:37.810 --> 00:08:41.229
By VE day Audie Murphy
Had endured three wounds and a nasty case

00:08:41.229 --> 00:08:44.250
of malaria,
Had been promoted three times, from private

00:08:44.250 --> 00:08:47.210
to officer
Had personally killed, wounded or captured

00:08:47.210 --> 00:08:53.430
about 240 enemy soldiers
And had received a total of 33 decorations

00:08:53.430 --> 00:08:58.850
from the US, French and Belgian militaries,
making him the most decorated soldier of WWII.

00:08:58.850 --> 00:09:03.720
But he had also lost his best friend Lattie,
as well as countless other army buddies.

00:09:03.720 --> 00:09:09.410
As he reflected immediately after the war
“There is VE-Day without, but no peace within.”

00:09:09.410 --> 00:09:43.370
…

00:09:43.370 --> 00:10:17.390
and

00:10:17.390 --> 00:10:29.170
Back

00:10:29.170 --> 00:10:33.820
Discharged from the Army on September 21,
1945, with the rank of 1st Lieutenant Audie

00:10:33.820 --> 00:10:38.320
had barely made it back home, when Hollywood
actor and producer James Cagney noticed his

00:10:38.320 --> 00:10:40.200
photo on the cover of a magazine.

00:10:40.200 --> 00:10:43.920
Cagney was a real Hollywood legend, who had
become an A-lister in the 1930s thanks to

00:10:43.920 --> 00:10:45.630
his roles in gangster movies.

00:10:45.630 --> 00:10:50.460
Cagney had an eye for recognising talent and
immediately noticed in Audie’s boyish face

00:10:50.460 --> 00:10:52.350
a certain star quality.

00:10:52.350 --> 00:10:55.180
This was a turning point in Audie Murphy’s
life.

00:10:55.180 --> 00:10:56.730
Was Cagney right?

00:10:56.730 --> 00:10:59.430
Well, not really, at least at the beginning.

00:10:59.430 --> 00:11:04.450
Hi company Cagney Productions paid for acting,
dancing and elocution lessons but in the following

00:11:04.450 --> 00:11:08.450
years Murphy struggled to make his ‘big
break’, managing to score only two minor

00:11:08.450 --> 00:11:13.000
roles, in Beyond Glory and Texas, Brooklyn
&amp; Heaven, both in 1948.

00:11:13.000 --> 00:11:16.920
If there is something we should have learned
about Murphy by now is that he was not easily

00:11:16.920 --> 00:11:17.920
discouraged.

00:11:17.920 --> 00:11:21.149
The following year, 1949, was a momentous
one for him.

00:11:21.149 --> 00:11:25.540
After much auditioning he finally got his
first lead role as a juvenile delinquent in

00:11:25.540 --> 00:11:26.540
Bad Boy.

00:11:26.540 --> 00:11:30.890
Fun fact – the film’s director, Kurt Neumann,
was more famous for directing the Tarzan series

00:11:30.890 --> 00:11:34.180
with Johnny Weissmuller and was a German from
Nuremberg.

00:11:34.180 --> 00:11:36.390
That must have been awkward.

00:11:36.390 --> 00:11:40.270
In addition to filming with Neumann, Audie
found time to publish his war memoire ‘To

00:11:40.270 --> 00:11:41.270
Hell and Back’.

00:11:41.270 --> 00:11:44.510
Written in a direct style, in the present
tense, Murphy’s book does not hold back

00:11:44.510 --> 00:11:47.350
from the most atrocious realities of modern
war.

00:11:47.350 --> 00:11:49.270
It would eventually become a best seller.

00:11:49.270 --> 00:11:52.870
Last but not least, 1949 was also the year
in which Audie got married.

00:11:52.870 --> 00:11:56.860
Just like Cagney had noticed Murphy on a magazine
cover, Audie had noticed his future wife,

00:11:56.860 --> 00:11:59.540
actress Wanda Hendrix, on the front page of
Coronet.

00:11:59.540 --> 00:12:03.230
Her beauty must have had the same effect of
being shelled by a Panzer.

00:12:03.230 --> 00:12:07.600
Audie insisted to arrange a meeting with her,
and after a short engagement in 1948, the

00:12:07.600 --> 00:12:11.910
two got married on the 8th of February 1949
and went on to act together in a Western film

00:12:11.910 --> 00:12:13.780
called Sierra in 1950.

00:12:13.780 --> 00:12:18.520
In 1951 Audie had the opportunity to be directed
by one of the greatest directors of all time,

00:12:18.520 --> 00:12:21.700
John Houston, in MGM's The Red Badge of Courage.

00:12:21.700 --> 00:12:25.330
This is now considered a classic but at the
time it bombed at the box office.

00:12:25.330 --> 00:12:29.709
As a result, Murphy, now under binding contract
with Universal-International Pictures, was

00:12:29.709 --> 00:12:34.149
assigned to star in a string of low-to-medium
budget Westerns, which was the genre which

00:12:34.149 --> 00:12:39.300
suited best Audie’s acting talent - especially
his easy going image and Texas drawl, which

00:12:39.300 --> 00:12:41.600
I am not going to imitate now.

00:12:41.600 --> 00:12:45.910
He acted in a total of forty-four films, being
the main star in thirty-nine of them.

00:12:45.910 --> 00:12:49.350
Despite most of these films have been forgotten
by now, it gave Murphy the opportunity to

00:12:49.350 --> 00:12:54.089
work with Hollywood royalty such as actor
Jimmy Stewart or director Don Siegel, of ‘Dirty

00:12:54.089 --> 00:12:55.209
Harry’ fame.

00:12:55.209 --> 00:12:59.090
However, the role for which he is best remembered
is … as himself!

00:12:59.090 --> 00:13:04.191
1955 in fact saw the release of "To Hell and
Back", adapted from his own war memoire, in

00:13:04.191 --> 00:13:08.540
which Audie Murphy the actor starred as Audie
Murphy, the war hero.

00:13:08.540 --> 00:13:12.279
‘To Hell and Back’ was a box office success
and remained Universal’s most profitable

00:13:12.279 --> 00:13:17.060
picture for 20 years, until ‘Jaws; by Spielberg
was released.

00:13:17.060 --> 00:13:21.540
It consecrated his status as a successful
actor and a household name, however it forced

00:13:21.540 --> 00:13:26.270
him to relive the most traumatic experiences
of his service in WWII.

00:13:26.270 --> 00:13:29.970
Back from Hell?

00:13:29.970 --> 00:13:36.070
I have to apologise now if I have made you
think that Audie’s life was all fine and

00:13:36.070 --> 00:13:38.170
dandy after returning from Germany.

00:13:38.170 --> 00:13:39.170
It wasn’t.

00:13:39.170 --> 00:13:43.090
Lieutenant Murphy had brought back with him
the demons of what had been called “shell

00:13:43.090 --> 00:13:48.130
shock”, later “combat fatigue” and nowadays
post-traumatic stress disorder, or PTSD.

00:13:48.130 --> 00:13:52.680
And Murphy displayed all the typical symptoms
of this condition, including nightmares, extreme

00:13:52.680 --> 00:13:55.250
anxiety, irritability and insomnia.

00:13:55.250 --> 00:13:58.590
This is how Audie described the agony and
misery he felt:

00:13:58.590 --> 00:14:02.959
“"It is as though a fire had roared through
this human house, leaving only the charred

00:14:02.959 --> 00:14:05.680
hulk of something that once was green."

00:14:05.680 --> 00:14:09.010
He woke up screaming at night and slept with
a loaded calibre 45 pistol nearby.

00:14:09.010 --> 00:14:13.160
He had a violent temper, so much so that in
a bar fight he inflicted such injuries on

00:14:13.160 --> 00:14:16.660
his opponent that he was almost convicted
of attempted murder.

00:14:16.660 --> 00:14:20.980
Don Siegel, who directed him in The Gun Runners
in 1958 reported that Audie often carried

00:14:20.980 --> 00:14:24.459
a gun on set, with many of the cast and crew
being afraid of him.

00:14:24.459 --> 00:14:27.660
His marriage with Wanda Hendrix suffered greatly
from his disorder.

00:14:27.660 --> 00:14:31.310
Audie was often beset with flashbacks and
paranoia in the first months after the wedding

00:14:31.310 --> 00:14:34.750
and often held Wanda at gunpoint during violent
episodes.

00:14:34.750 --> 00:14:39.580
This must have degenerated in pathologic jealousy,
as Murphy demanded from his wife – by then

00:14:39.580 --> 00:14:43.000
a much more successful actress than he was
– that she gave up her job to be a stay-at-home

00:14:43.000 --> 00:14:44.000
wife.

00:14:44.000 --> 00:14:47.550
Wanda was frightened by this behaviour and
left him after only seven months of marriage,

00:14:47.550 --> 00:14:49.209
charging him with mental cruelty.

00:14:49.209 --> 00:14:52.690
The final decree of divorce came on April
14, 1950.

00:14:52.690 --> 00:14:56.300
Murphy married again the following year with
Pamela Archer, also an actress.

00:14:56.300 --> 00:14:59.900
This union would be a happier one, lasting
until Audie’s death.

00:14:59.900 --> 00:15:04.120
But his troubles with insomnia and depression
continued throughout the 1960s, in parallel

00:15:04.120 --> 00:15:06.209
with his declining film career.

00:15:06.209 --> 00:15:10.380
In the end he became addicted to a powerful
prescription drug called Placidyl which actually

00:15:10.380 --> 00:15:12.490
can worsen a pre-existing state of depression.

00:15:12.490 --> 00:15:15.490
Audie Murphy, though, was never one to raise
a white flag.

00:15:15.490 --> 00:15:19.370
Realizing he had become addicted to Placidyl,
he locked himself in a motel room for a week,

00:15:19.370 --> 00:15:22.850
while he went full cold turkey and fought
back the withdrawal symptoms.

00:15:22.850 --> 00:15:26.420
He ended up beating the addiction and went
on to break the taboo of talking about the

00:15:26.420 --> 00:15:29.890
mental disorders many soldiers suffered when
they returned home.

00:15:29.890 --> 00:15:34.750
His willingness to do so opened up for the
first time Nation-wide discussions about psychological

00:15:34.750 --> 00:15:39.230
care for Korean and Vietnam war veterans upon
their return to the US.

00:15:39.230 --> 00:15:43.440
Meanwhile, the studio system that Murphy grew
into as an actor crumbled.

00:15:43.440 --> 00:15:47.450
Universal's new owners, MCA turned the studio's
focus toward the now more lucrative television

00:15:47.450 --> 00:15:48.450
industry.

00:15:48.450 --> 00:15:51.610
For theatrical productions, it dropped its
roster of contract players and hired actors

00:15:51.610 --> 00:15:53.240
on a per-picture basis only.

00:15:53.240 --> 00:15:57.170
What this meant for Murphy is that he now
had to audition and hustle for each role,

00:15:57.170 --> 00:15:58.990
just like at the beginning of his career.

00:15:58.990 --> 00:16:02.740
This was made even more difficult by the fact
that the traditional, mid-budget Westerns

00:16:02.740 --> 00:16:06.480
he had become famous for, they were now a
thing of the past.

00:16:06.480 --> 00:16:09.820
The 1960s were the age of counter-culture
and the Frontier was now represented by the

00:16:09.820 --> 00:16:13.020
new wave of European-made ‘Spaghetti Westerns’.

00:16:13.020 --> 00:16:17.170
Audie tried his hand at the new genre, landing
the leading role in ‘The Texican’, in

00:16:17.170 --> 00:16:21.329
1966, which is more precisely a “Paella
Western”, as the majority of the cast was

00:16:21.329 --> 00:16:22.329
Spanish.

00:16:22.329 --> 00:16:26.010
Apparently, it is not as terrible as it sounds
– but it tanked at the box office.

00:16:26.010 --> 00:16:30.430
As a soldier, he had garnered 33 medals; as
an actor the only official accolade was a

00:16:30.430 --> 00:16:34.570
1955 prize as “Movie Distributors’ Favourite
Western Actor”.

00:16:34.570 --> 00:16:38.660
Fortunately, Murphy had diversified his activities
becoming also a rancher and businessman in

00:16:38.660 --> 00:16:41.190
the trade of breeding and selling thoroughbred
horses.

00:16:41.190 --> 00:16:45.370
He had also become a songwriter, writing the
lyrics to many pop and country hits of the

00:16:45.370 --> 00:16:46.550
1960s.

00:16:46.550 --> 00:16:50.740
His most famous song, Shutters and Boards,
was recorded by legendary crooner Dean Martin

00:16:50.740 --> 00:16:53.160
among others and was even covered in Swedish.

00:16:53.160 --> 00:16:57.420
It is easy to find in the lyrics an echo of
the failed romance with Wanda.

00:16:57.420 --> 00:17:01.500
Now, I’d love to play it for you but unfortunately
my guitar is being re-strung, so I will just

00:17:01.500 --> 00:17:03.730
read you some lines:
“Shutters and boards cover the windows

00:17:03.730 --> 00:17:08.149
Of the house where we used to live
All I have left is a heart full of sorrow

00:17:08.149 --> 00:17:12.449
Since she said she'd never forgive
The house that we built was once filled with

00:17:12.449 --> 00:17:16.100
laughter
But I changed that laughter to tears

00:17:16.100 --> 00:17:21.050
Now I must live in a world without sunshine
Oh how I wish you were here”

00:17:21.050 --> 00:17:29.480
From Hell to Heaven
On May 28th, 1971, Murphy was flying on a

00:17:29.480 --> 00:17:31.570
private plane, on his way to a business meeting.

00:17:31.570 --> 00:17:35.130
According to many accounts, by this point
he had largely lost his fortune to gambling

00:17:35.130 --> 00:17:36.130
and bad investments.

00:17:36.130 --> 00:17:40.519
Perhaps this meeting was to set him back on
the good track, but we will never now.

00:17:40.519 --> 00:17:45.679
The plane ran into thick fog over Craig County,
Virginia, near Roanoke, and crashed into the

00:17:45.679 --> 00:17:46.909
side of a mountain.

00:17:46.909 --> 00:17:49.799
Audie and all five other passengers and crew
were killed.

00:17:49.799 --> 00:17:52.639
He was buried with full military honours at
Arlington National Military Cemetery, on June

00:17:52.639 --> 00:17:53.639
the 7th.

00:17:53.639 --> 00:17:57.850
According to cemetery records, the only grave
site visited by more people than that of Murphy

00:17:57.850 --> 00:17:59.509
is that of President John F. Kennedy

